1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,552
This is not a quiz.

2
00:00:01,552 --> 00:00:06,611
It is the same five-question spot check from Section 7.4, aimed at the thing you

3
00:00:06,611 --> 00:00:09,268
just built instead of one of Priya's four.

4
00:00:09,268 --> 00:00:14,328
Answer each one against your actual capstone, not against how you hope it turned

5
00:00:14,328 --> 00:00:14,581
out.

6
00:00:14,581 --> 00:00:19,577
Get a coffee, read your own system message the way a stranger would, and answer

7
00:00:19,577 --> 00:00:20,146
honestly.

8
00:00:20,146 --> 00:00:21,854
Nobody grades this but you.

9
00:00:22,404 --> 00:00:24,412
Five rows, five honest answers.

10
00:00:24,412 --> 00:00:27,779
A "not yet" on any of them is not a failed capstone.

11
00:00:27,779 --> 00:00:32,960
It is exactly the kind of finding a spot check exists to produce, and the fix is

12
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:38,335
usually small: go back to the specific lecture that row maps to and finish that one

13
00:00:38,335 --> 00:00:38,658
step.

14
00:00:38,658 --> 00:00:43,904
Most learners land a clean pass on three rows and a genuine "not yet" on one, and

15
00:00:43,904 --> 00:00:48,890
that single gap is usually more useful than five easy passes would have been.

16
00:00:48,890 --> 00:00:51,092
The map back is one row deep each.

17
00:00:51,092 --> 00:00:56,467
Job description gaps go to 1.4, and usually mean the ceiling line is missing rather

18
00:00:56,467 --> 00:00:58,021
than the whole document.

19
00:00:58,021 --> 00:01:03,202
Scope gaps go to 1.3 and 3.2: open the trigger and read which folder it actually

20
00:01:03,202 --> 00:01:03,590
names.

21
00:01:03,590 --> 00:01:08,836
Judgement gaps go to 4.3's escape hatches, the commonest miss by far, because "do

22
00:01:08,836 --> 00:01:12,980
not invent" feels implied until you watch a model fill a vacuum.

23
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:18,161
Trial gaps go to 7.1, and honest-number gaps to 9.2, where the checking time you

24
00:01:18,161 --> 00:01:20,622
forgot to subtract is waiting for you.

25
00:01:20,622 --> 00:01:25,155
Twenty minutes in the right lecture beats an evening of general worry.

26
00:01:25,705 --> 00:01:30,378
That question is the same one this entire course has been building toward since

27
00:01:30,378 --> 00:01:32,035
Section 1's job description.

28
00:01:32,035 --> 00:01:36,235
It never goes away, no matter how many agents you build after this one.

29
00:01:36,235 --> 00:01:40,376
Notice it is just the trust ladder, asked at the moment of graduation.

30
00:01:40,376 --> 00:01:45,345
Answering no is not a confession, it is a reading of which rung you are actually on,

31
00:01:45,345 --> 00:01:50,255
and the course has spent five sections teaching you that standing on the right rung

32
00:01:50,255 --> 00:01:52,148
matters more than climbing fast.

33
00:01:52,148 --> 00:01:56,940
The learners who get the most from this method are the ones who can say "not yet"

34
00:01:56,940 --> 00:02:00,667
out loud, cheaply, before reality says it for them expensively.

35
00:02:00,667 --> 00:02:04,808
If that sentence sounds like Priya's out-loud double check, it should.

36
00:02:04,808 --> 00:02:09,304
That habit was the whole curriculum, wearing different clothes each section.

37
00:02:09,854 --> 00:02:11,116
And date your scores.

38
00:02:11,116 --> 00:02:16,103
A rubric filled in today is also a baseline for the day you re-run it, three months

39
00:02:16,103 --> 00:02:20,850
from now, on the same agent after its briefing has been tuned and its trial has

40
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:21,811
grown a history.

41
00:02:21,811 --> 00:02:26,738
Watching your own "not yet" turn into a dated pass is the most convincing evidence

42
00:02:26,738 --> 00:02:29,622
this course can offer you that the method holds.

43
00:02:29,622 --> 00:02:30,644
One lecture left.

44
00:02:30,644 --> 00:02:33,107
Thank you, and where this goes from here.
